# Crm: The Developer Bible

Welcome to the **Crm** Developer Bible. This guide is your single source of truth for understanding the architecture, deep-dive mechanisms, and coding standards of this project.

The solution is built on **.NET 10.0** utilizing a strict **Clean Architecture** combined with a **Vertical Slice** arrangement within the Application layer.

---

## 🏗️ 1. Architecture Flow & Project Connections

The foundation of the project relies on **Dependency Inversion**; inner layers never depend on outer layers.

*   `Crm.Domain`: The heart. **No dependencies**.
*   `Crm.Application`: Depends purely on [Domain](file:///d:/Projects/Saay/SaayAI/Crm8-sql/Crm/src/Crm.Domain/Common/BaseEntity.cs#14-18). Defines the "What" (Interfaces, Commands, Queries).
*   `Crm.Infrastructure`: Depends on [Application](file:///d:/Projects/Saay/SaayAI/Crm8-sql/Crm/src/Crm.Infrastructure/Persistence/ApplicationDbContext.cs#14-21). Defines the "How" (SQL Server, EF Core, Redis, Identity).
*   `Crm.Api`: Depends on `Infrastructure` and [Application](file:///d:/Projects/Saay/SaayAI/Crm8-sql/Crm/src/Crm.Infrastructure/Persistence/ApplicationDbContext.cs#14-21). Assembles the app, registers DI, and exposes HTTP Endpoints.

**Dependency Injection at API Startup:**
The API project's [Program.cs](file:///d:/Projects/Saay/SaayAI/Crm8-sql/Crm/src/Crm.Api/Program.cs) registers layers by calling respective static extension methods:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddApplicationServices();      // From Application project (Registers MediatR, FluentValidation)
builder.Services.AddInfrastructureServices(...); // From Infrastructure project (Registers EF, Identity, Hangfire)
```

